This builds directly on the bell-curve example you just saw. The only change: instead of one flat μ and σ for all bonds, a model predicts μ and σ from each bond's features, and we train it in a way that uses your losses too, not just your wins. The final win-probability step is the exact same Φ(z) you already understand.
I'll write the target as "level relative to a reference" so higher always means a higher bid (and higher win probability), keeping it consistent with the example.

What the code is doing. For any bond, it estimates the distribution of the level you have to beat — the best competing bid — expressed relative to a reference price. Your win probability is just how much of that distribution sits below your bid, read off the normal CDF. Training is where all the work happens: the model learns how the center and spread of the competition shift with the bond's characteristics (a liquid AAA benchmark has competition tightly bunched just under the eval; a beat-up odd-lot has competition that's lower and far more scattered).
Why the censored likelihood (the important part). Look at the two branches in the loss function — that's the whole methodology in two lines. On a win, you saw the cover, so it's an exact data point and the model is rewarded for placing high probability density right there. On a loss, you don't know where competition was, only that it was above your bid, so the model is rewarded for placing probability mass above that level. This is the part a plain win/loss classifier gets wrong: it effectively treats a loss as "competition = my bid," which is false and biases your competition estimate low. Here, losses contribute honest information without being thrown away or distorted.
Why "level relative to reference." Subtracting the reference makes bonds comparable — a level of "−0.30 vs BVAL" means roughly the same competitive aggression on a 5-year AA as on a 12-year A, whereas a raw price of 99.7 means nothing across different bonds. In production you'd do this subtraction in yield or spread (or DV01-adjusted) units rather than dollar price, since a quarter-point means very different things at 2 years vs 20 years — but the code structure is identical, you just change what ref and the difference are measured in.
The learner is swappable; the loss is the spine. The model(X) → mu, sigma box can be gradient boosting with a custom objective, NGBoost (which predicts distribution parameters out of the box), or a small neural net — anything that can output two numbers and be trained on a custom loss. What makes it this method rather than a generic regression is the censored likelihood. A simpler first cut holds σ constant and only lets μ vary with features; the better version predicts σ(X) too, which is what captures illiquid bonds having wider, less predictable competition.
Two things to add before trusting the probabilities. First, calibration: after training, check on held-out data that bids the model calls 30%-likely actually win about 30% of the time, and apply a conformal or isotonic correction if they don't — a probability is only useful if it's honest. Second, validation must be walk-forward by date (train on the past, test on the future) because muni regimes drift, and score it with log-loss or pinball loss plus a calibration plot rather than plain accuracy.
In one sentence: it's the bell-curve example with the mean and spread predicted per-bond, and trained so that every win teaches it exactly where competition was and every loss teaches it that competition was at least as high as you bid.
